WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.268
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:08.849
Hello and welcome to our conference call,
MBA Admissions Insights,

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:10.850
Being a Great Applicant.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:11.898
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:11.898 --> 00:00:13.873
My name is Ari Chasnoff.

00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:18.120
I'm the communications manager
in the MBA Admissions Office.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.737
I'm joined by my colleague, Lizabeth
Cutler, an Associate Director here.

00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:23.372
&gt;&gt; Hi everybody.

00:00:23.372 --> 00:00:27.026
&gt;&gt; So in the next 30 minutes, we'll be
talking about steps you can take to be

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:31.540
a great candidate for the Stanford
Graduate School of Business MBA program.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.070
Then we'll answer some of the questions
you submitted in advance.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.859
First let's discuss what
a great candidate even means.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.540
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.450
For us, that's about identifying people
who will be great students while they're

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.870
here on campus, and
who will have a meaningful and

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.320
positive impact wherever
they go once they graduate.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:56.396
&gt;&gt; So what steps do you think people
can take to be a stronger candidate?

00:00:56.396 --> 00:01:00.794
&gt;&gt; Here are some thoughts based on
interesting questions people have asked me

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:01.780
over the years.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.933
There's a lot of different
ways people think about this.

00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:08.528
One is focus more on being a great
candidate than creating a great

00:01:08.528 --> 00:01:10.390
application.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.497
Believe it or not,
we want to make the process as simple and

00:01:13.497 --> 00:01:15.067
efficient as we can for you.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:20.498
And we only ask for what we really
need to know to create a great class.

00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:24.920
That said, I know the application process
takes so much of your time and energy.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.490
Like anything you really want,
it takes hard work and

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.420
a lot of heart to be successful at it.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.150
But sometimes I get the impression
when I'm talking to people

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.010
that someone's letting the application
process take over their life.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:41.400
So if you spend every extra minute in
your year leading up to the application

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.730
studying for the GMAT or
the GRE, what aren't you doing?

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.350
So for example, given the choice between
taking on a great new project and

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.310
spending 24/7 on your application,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:56.280
take on the project, it will give
your recommender more to talk about.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.941
It will give you the kind of growth
opportunity that will help you become

00:01:59.941 --> 00:02:01.721
a better student once you arrive.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:06.154
&gt;&gt; Lizabeth, I've heard people ask, I've
got an exiting new job opportunity doing

00:02:06.154 --> 00:02:10.583
something I've always wanted to do, but
everyone's telling me to stay in the same

00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:13.770
job until after I get into school,
even though I'm bored.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.910
&gt;&gt; Yep, I've heard that one too.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.600
A great candidate for
us jumps on opportunities.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.030
If you're ready and
excited about it, do it.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.990
Don't do it to impress us, we're perfectly
happy that you stay in your current job

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.620
for whatever amount of time
that makes the most sense.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.280
What I'm talking about is someone
who's ready for a change, but

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.440
using the application process
as an excuse not to do it, you

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.940
really want to get out and live your life,
and that makes you a great candidate.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.686
Obviously, I want you to put the time and
effort in for a strong application,

00:02:40.686 --> 00:02:43.396
what I'm talking about is
knowing when to draw the line.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:48.292
What's the marginal benefit, that's a word
you learn maybe after becoming an MBA for

00:02:48.292 --> 00:02:50.972
some of you,
of taking the GRE one more time, or

00:02:50.972 --> 00:02:53.066
rewriting your essay one more time.

00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:56.466
If you think at that point, you can
really improve either your score, or

00:02:56.466 --> 00:02:59.990
your essay, or your application,
definitely spend that time.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:03.220
But, if you reach that point where you
know, deep down, you've done your best,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.090
and you're just worrying over it, move on.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.830
You know,
we think you should follow your interests,

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.060
take the risk to try leading someone,
or leading something.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.880
Learn from your successes and failures.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.780
And apply that learning into
doing it better the next time.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.890
Step up for challenges and
do an excellent job.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.970
Those are the things that will show
up in your recommendations and

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.590
show up in who you are and get us excited.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.770
&gt;&gt; So, your're saying,
do what you feel strongly about,

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.750
not just what people tell you will
help you get into business school.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.470
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.130
Don't let anyone else
tell you what we want, or

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.230
that you should change your career
decisions which are so important and

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:43.280
so personal around
the application process.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.630
You know yourself best.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:48.410
If you want to know what we want,
come ask us.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:49.970
That's why we travel around the world.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:51.070
We do this webinar.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.170
We answer the phones and
we post information on our website.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.970
Always go to the source,
whether that's us or

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.820
another school, about what
the school is really looking for.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.420
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, great.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.940
What else do you think could
help people be a good candidate?

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.640
&gt;&gt; Well, so one thing is we hope
you show us your best self.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.640
Just make sure it's your self,
that it's really you being yourself

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.450
as opposed to what you think
we want you to sound like or

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.100
what other people are telling
you you should sound like.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.630
And the other is, I think the best thing
you can do is really research the schools,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.080
just like you're doing by
being on this call today.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.690
Know why you want to come to Stanford.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.900
What would you get from this experience?

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.790
And what makes it a good fit for you?

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.630
Because then you narrow down and
only apply to the schools

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:38.970
that are really good options for you, and
that'll show up in your application too.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:43.191
&gt;&gt; So, if I am a great candidate
what else do I need to do

00:04:43.191 --> 00:04:45.657
to actually demonstrate it?

00:04:45.657 --> 00:04:50.010
Are there logistics of
submitting an application?

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.217
How are those important?

00:04:51.217 --> 00:04:55.283
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I get the impression that
sometimes people spend too much time on

00:04:55.283 --> 00:04:57.440
some things and miss others.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.350
So here's some examples of
things people may miss.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.600
One is just following instructions,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.130
literally making sure you've read through
the instructions and follow them.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.540
Another is to be aware of the deadlines.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.160
Nothing that is submitted
after the deadline counts,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.910
because we start reading
applications as soon as we get them.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:18.380
So very often people will try to submit
information like another test score or

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:23.700
another recommendation or things like that
after the deadline and we don't accept it.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.610
The other thing is to review
the entire application ahead of time so

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.920
you know what to expect.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.010
So I think sometimes people will work on
the things they know about like I need to

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.680
get a letter of reference, but they won't
look at all the different sections of

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.170
the application and make sure they've
got everything that they need.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.020
And so I think that's important.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.870
And the last thought on that is to
make sure you're putting your best

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:45.770
foot forward and presenting yourself well.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.354
But be true and
factual about your experience.

00:05:48.354 --> 00:05:51.735
We do validate the information you
submit before you can become a student,

00:05:51.735 --> 00:05:55.040
and we can rescind an offer if
you've misrepresented yourself.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.172
So you do want to be careful about that.

00:05:58.172 --> 00:06:01.708
&gt;&gt; There are all kinds of great students,
some inside the classroom,

00:06:01.708 --> 00:06:06.052
some in extracurricular activities, or
in other contributions to their class and

00:06:06.052 --> 00:06:06.748
the school.

00:06:06.748 --> 00:06:10.790
There's really no one mold for
the perfect Stanford MBA student.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:14.850
&gt;&gt; So true, and yet
they do have some things in common.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.480
So let's quickly review
our evaluation criteria.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:21.080
We'll just touch on this briefly since you
can learn more about this in our website.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.570
&gt;&gt; Right so
the first one is intellectual vitality.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.609
What are we looking for there Lizabeth?

00:06:26.609 --> 00:06:29.970
&gt;&gt; The way I'd really encourage you
to think about this is Stanford is

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:30.877
a vibrant place.

00:06:30.877 --> 00:06:35.743
It's a place to solve big problems and
take intellectual risks.

00:06:35.743 --> 00:06:40.256
So, were looking someone that is
intellectually engage in whatever the one

00:06:40.256 --> 00:06:43.012
or many topics are that
catch your attention.

00:06:43.012 --> 00:06:44.340
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:47.920
Next for evaluation criteria is
demonstrated leadership potential.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.050
What is the reason behind this one?

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:53.250
&gt;&gt; Well, we integrate graduates
students for increase ability to lead.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:54.650
You've seen our model?

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.360
Change lives, change organizations,
and change the world.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.680
It's the only way you can go out and
create change is if you can actually lead.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:04.070
So we're both looking for
what skills you may have now and

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.020
how open you are to learning new ones.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.760
Do you know what makes you a good leader?

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.200
Do you know what you
aren't good at at all?

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.320
It's okay if you don't have
much experience yet, but

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.680
a great candidate is at least
performing well at work and in school.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.470
So they're likely to be in a position
to advance later in their career and

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.040
be able to lead.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.520
&gt;&gt; Okay, well the third criteria we
consider is personal qualities and

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.750
contributions.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.020
What do you mean by that?

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.830
&gt;&gt; This is the hardest one to explain.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.589
There's no right or wrong answers to this,

00:07:30.589 --> 00:07:33.782
it's a way of saying that we're interested
Interested in getting to know you.

00:07:33.782 --> 00:07:37.832
We don't seek to put people in boxes and
don't have any quotas or anything.

00:07:37.832 --> 00:07:41.592
So we look at each individual we consider
what they might gain from our program and

00:07:41.592 --> 00:07:43.600
how they might contribute to the program.

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:45.690
&gt;&gt; Fantastic.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:49.430
So now let's move on to answering
some questions from participants.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:54.306
Thank you all for submitting so
many questions in advance.

00:07:54.306 --> 00:07:57.994
So here's a question we received.

00:07:57.994 --> 00:08:01.837
Many people talk about the importance
of distinguishing oneself.

00:08:01.837 --> 00:08:04.962
&gt;&gt; From the majority in
the application process.

00:08:04.962 --> 00:08:09.864
With regard to the essay questions,
what are some of the most common

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:14.946
mistakes made by the majority of
the applicants who are not admitted?

00:08:14.946 --> 00:08:18.367
&gt;&gt; So the answer to this changes
year to year, of course.

00:08:18.367 --> 00:08:22.866
I would say these days, the main one is
pushing your own agenda rather than just

00:08:22.866 --> 00:08:25.030
answering the question we ask.

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:28.498
And frankly, even some of the people
we admit are doing this or

00:08:28.498 --> 00:08:29.774
making this mistake.

00:08:29.774 --> 00:08:35.109
So be really careful about the advice that
you take on essays even if it's someone

00:08:35.109 --> 00:08:39.972
who got in, because you don't know if
the choices the made contributed to

00:08:39.972 --> 00:08:45.250
them getting in or we're in obstacle
to our making a decision to admit them.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.613
Really, the main thing for
you to do is take the time to reflect.

00:08:48.613 --> 00:08:50.226
Read the question carefully.

00:08:50.226 --> 00:08:54.316
Reflect on the true answer for
yourself and then spend the time trying to

00:08:54.316 --> 00:08:58.136
articulate your answer rather than
thinking about anyone else and

00:08:58.136 --> 00:09:02.023
distinguishing yourself from anyone else,
because you don't know who

00:09:02.023 --> 00:09:07.130
else is applying and what they're saying
and that's just about impossible to do.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:11.664
&gt;&gt; Second question, how would you evaluate
leadership potential in candidates,

00:09:11.664 --> 00:09:16.075
whose professional rolls don't give
them opportunities to demonstrate it?

00:09:16.075 --> 00:09:21.272
For example, in entry level positions or
positions where they don't need a team or

00:09:21.272 --> 00:09:24.032
if they have a nontraditional background.

00:09:24.032 --> 00:09:27.596
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think this is
an important question, because so

00:09:27.596 --> 00:09:32.477
many of our candidates don't actually
have a leadership experience yet or

00:09:32.477 --> 00:09:34.650
anything very formal.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:37.800
It's not important, you don't have to
have work experience coming in and

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.060
you don't specifically have to
have leadership experience.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.033
We're looking at the qualities
that you do have and

00:09:44.033 --> 00:09:47.709
how they might contribute to
your effectiveness, as leader.

00:09:47.709 --> 00:09:51.751
We're looking at your self-awareness,
because a good leader is able to learn

00:09:51.751 --> 00:09:55.270
from situations around them and
adjust their behavior and actions.

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:00.890
Those are some of the ways I would start
looking at it and the other thing is

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.710
don't get tripped up thinking, we're only
looking for formal leadership experience.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:09.017
It might be how you helped someone
next to you at work or something.

00:10:09.017 --> 00:10:12.207
Many, many candidates are coming
from situations where they don't

00:10:12.207 --> 00:10:13.970
have leadership opportunities yet.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:19.690
&gt;&gt; Here's a question
about a candidate's GPA.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:24.130
How can applicants confident in their
academic ability, but with a poor

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:29.160
undergraduate GPA demonstrate that their
intellectual capacity, discipline and

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.920
academic rigor is not limited to
their past academic performance?

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.618
So, we're not evaluating your GPA alone.

00:10:38.618 --> 00:10:40.602
We're looking at your whole transcript.

00:10:40.602 --> 00:10:43.949
We're looking at things you've done
since you finished undergraduate,

00:10:43.949 --> 00:10:45.390
if you're not still a student.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:51.756
So the question I'd ask you is what
leads you to believe that your,

00:10:51.756 --> 00:10:55.963
let's see, your intellectual capacity,

00:10:55.963 --> 00:11:01.980
your discipline and
rigor are different than your GPA shows?

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.102
What evidence can you
demonstrate to show that to us,

00:11:04.102 --> 00:11:05.809
because that's what we're looking for.

00:11:05.809 --> 00:11:08.347
We want the evidence that you
can succeed in the classroom and

00:11:08.347 --> 00:11:10.108
that you contribute to your classmates.

00:11:10.108 --> 00:11:12.100
So, that's what you want to think about.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.173
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:18.916
So here's another question, what are the
best ways to engage as an applicant from

00:11:18.916 --> 00:11:23.690
abroad to learn more about Stanford and
help Stanford get to know me?

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:24.883
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely a challenge.

00:11:24.883 --> 00:11:25.900
So first of all,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:30.426
I should make it very clear that we
don't expect you to come visit campus.

00:11:30.426 --> 00:11:35.391
Anything you can do to learn about
us is great, but it's all for you,

00:11:35.391 --> 00:11:39.410
so you can make a good
educated decision about us.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:43.537
We're not a evaluating you based on
whether you've been involved with us or

00:11:43.537 --> 00:11:44.707
attended anything.

00:11:44.707 --> 00:11:48.889
That said, we are travelling literally
all over the world this summer and

00:11:48.889 --> 00:11:50.449
take a look at our website.

00:11:50.449 --> 00:11:54.930
See if we're coming somewhere near you or
close enough that you can get, so

00:11:54.930 --> 00:12:00.460
you can meet some alumni and an officer in
person and really learn about the program.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:03.705
We encourage you to do that and
then really, it's our website.

00:12:03.705 --> 00:12:04.735
Dive in.

00:12:04.735 --> 00:12:08.551
Ari's created amazing opportunities to
learn more about the program there,

00:12:08.551 --> 00:12:10.470
like a day in the life and other things.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:14.980
So, really focus on how you
can learn about Stanford.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:19.587
You don't need to do anything separate in
order to help Stanford get to know you,

00:12:19.587 --> 00:12:21.564
that's really the application.

00:12:21.564 --> 00:12:25.810
So, the only way we're getting to know
candidates is through the application.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.899
We don't expect people to do anything else
and to keep it as fair as possible, we're

00:12:29.899 --> 00:12:34.049
evaluating candidates based, specifically
on their application not on any outside

00:12:34.049 --> 00:12:37.870
factors like, whether you're coming
to campus, whether you know anybody.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:38.400
None of that.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:40.480
&gt;&gt; Fantastic.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:46.930
Next question, does it matter which age or
point in life I'm in?

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:50.059
I have a vision and
direction I want to go in, but

00:12:50.059 --> 00:12:55.242
I don't know what it's going to look like
in terms of job function or company.

00:12:55.242 --> 00:12:57.396
&gt;&gt; This is a little harder to answer.

00:12:57.396 --> 00:13:01.462
It doesn't matter what age or
point of life you're in to us,

00:13:01.462 --> 00:13:03.549
it absolutely matters to you.

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:06.228
And so
the way it matters is you really want to

00:13:06.228 --> 00:13:09.577
be thoughtful about why you
want to be in the program and

00:13:09.577 --> 00:13:13.760
what you want to get out of it, and
be able to articulate that for us.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.645
We're completely neutral about the where
you are part, it's more about where you

00:13:18.645 --> 00:13:22.227
are and where you want to get and
how we can help you with that.

00:13:22.227 --> 00:13:25.491
So if you have a general vision and
direction you want to go in,

00:13:25.491 --> 00:13:29.267
then help us understand what that
direction is and why you think coming

00:13:29.267 --> 00:13:33.260
here will help and why the MBA program,
specifically will help.

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:37.113
You definitely do not need to know
what exact job function or company.

00:13:37.113 --> 00:13:40.225
You'll notice our essay two
question is more about Stanford,

00:13:40.225 --> 00:13:42.710
it's not about your short and
long-term goals.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.590
We don't ask that, because we know it's
going to change as soon as you get into

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.435
the application process and
certainly by the time you're a student.

00:13:48.435 --> 00:13:53.097
And so we think that those longer
term visions and aspirations are more

00:13:53.097 --> 00:13:58.010
likely to remain stable, and
that's what we're really interested in.

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.380
&gt;&gt; Well, here's an interesting question.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:04.330
Someone is asking,
can you help us understand what

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:07.540
happens behind the scenes
after I submit my application?

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:10.666
&gt;&gt; Sure.
The key things that you need to know,

00:14:10.666 --> 00:14:15.130
really are first of all, it's a real human
being that's reading your application.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:16.252
It's not an algorithm.

00:14:16.252 --> 00:14:19.624
I've had people who create algorithms for

00:14:19.624 --> 00:14:23.750
a living not believe me,
but it is actually a fact.

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:25.885
So it's a real human being, it's me or

00:14:25.885 --> 00:14:29.136
someone like me that's interested
in getting to know you.

00:14:29.136 --> 00:14:32.815
Our readers are not faculty or students.

00:14:32.815 --> 00:14:37.943
They're all paid staff and it's
a well-trained, well-calibrated group.

00:14:37.943 --> 00:14:40.134
So, there's no randomness in here.

00:14:40.134 --> 00:14:45.350
It's a professional, a group of
people reading your application.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:48.097
We read it and we decide whether
you'll make it to interview.

00:14:48.097 --> 00:14:52.626
And ultimately, whether those
interviewed will receive an offer.

00:14:52.626 --> 00:14:55.988
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:14:55.988 --> 00:15:00.009
The next question is how
do I strike a good balance

00:15:00.009 --> 00:15:04.227
between communicating
the value I'd receive from

00:15:04.227 --> 00:15:09.630
the program versus the value
I can contribute to peers?

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.188
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,
that's a really great question.

00:15:11.188 --> 00:15:14.736
I'm so glad you're even thinking about it.

00:15:14.736 --> 00:15:19.382
I don't even know how to answer it,
because, I mean,

00:15:19.382 --> 00:15:23.826
all you need to do is answer
the questions we asked and

00:15:23.826 --> 00:15:27.990
that's not specifically
a question we'd ask.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.114
So I think it's really,

00:15:30.114 --> 00:15:35.168
especially up to you to focus on
what you are here to learn and

00:15:35.168 --> 00:15:41.452
I think we'll pretty much assess what
value we think you can contribute.

00:15:41.452 --> 00:15:44.065
It's not a question that we ask.

00:15:44.065 --> 00:15:46.670
So but really,
great that you're thinking about it and

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.090
I hope that you're contributing to
the people around you wherever you are and

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:51.640
that also makes you a great candidate.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:53.273
&gt;&gt; Great point.

00:15:53.273 --> 00:15:57.624
So, the next question is about
the three evaluation criteria.

00:15:57.624 --> 00:15:59.253
I know we recently discussed.

00:15:59.253 --> 00:16:03.466
The GSB website talks about the importance
of three criteria, intellectual vitality,

00:16:03.466 --> 00:16:08.170
demonstrated leadership potential,
personal qualities and contributions.

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:12.400
Where is the best place to communicate
these in the application process?

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:19.646
&gt;&gt; Yeah, again,
one thing doesn't correlate to another.

00:16:19.646 --> 00:16:23.447
Those are the things that I keep in
mind as I'm evaluating every section of

00:16:23.447 --> 00:16:24.990
the application.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:29.770
So you might see more intellectual
vitality showing up in your transcript,

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:32.270
where we'll really looking
at what you studied and why.

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.955
But truthfully, it can come come
up anywhere in the application and

00:16:34.955 --> 00:16:37.945
the same is true for
all the other criteria.

00:16:37.945 --> 00:16:41.675
So they don't map to specific
locations in the application.

00:16:42.865 --> 00:16:45.995
Really, they're things that shine through,
can shine through anywhere and

00:16:45.995 --> 00:16:48.065
it really depends on you and your answers.

00:16:49.165 --> 00:16:49.675
&gt;&gt; Fantastic.

00:16:51.025 --> 00:16:56.819
Well, we got a high volume of
questions submitted in advance.

00:16:56.819 --> 00:17:00.670
So we have about 13 minutes left.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:03.410
So let's move on to some
additional questions.

00:17:04.895 --> 00:17:10.340
Lizabeth, how would you recommend
a candidate reference personal or

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:14.910
professional relationships with
GSB alumni in their application?

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.072
Is there a special way
to go about doing that?

00:17:18.072 --> 00:17:22.420
&gt;&gt; Well no, I guess I'm wondering
what's behind the question.

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.970
Is it that you're trying to demonstrate
that you know somebody here?

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:31.250
If that's the case, you don't need to.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.330
And I don't necessarily even
encourage you to do so.

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:35.030
It's not a requirement.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:36.850
It's not one of our criteria.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:42.030
If theres something you learned,
if there's a reason, so for example,

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:47.490
if the GSB alums you've met
are the reason you're applying.

00:17:47.490 --> 00:17:49.730
Or there's something about them
that stands out to you and

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.640
that's part of your decision process.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:56.039
Then that could be relevant for
you to share, but otherwise,

00:17:56.039 --> 00:17:59.945
I don't recommend you go out
of your way to do it at all.

00:17:59.945 --> 00:18:03.780
I've had people ask me for a reference,
someone I know really well and

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:08.400
they thought well, as an alum it would
be really helpful to hear from you.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:12.670
And I actually suggest they not waste
their time, so really focus on getting

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:19.080
references from people who know you well
and can speak to your performance at work.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.580
And don't worry about the alumni factor.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:28.900
&gt;&gt; Great, next question is
about reviewing one's essays.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:34.765
What is the recommended process to review
and re-review your written statement?

00:18:34.765 --> 00:18:36.398
How would you recommend going about that?

00:18:36.398 --> 00:18:40.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we have no recommendation,
it's totally your own process.

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:45.840
Some tips I've heard from
people are things like you can

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:51.610
give it to someone who knows
you really well and say hey,

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:55.150
if you read this essay,
would you know it was me?

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:56.170
Does it sound like me?

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:59.260
That's a way to make sure it's
really your authentic voice.

00:19:00.325 --> 00:19:04.230
Another thing you can do is you can give
them the essay without the question on

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:07.490
it and ask them today,
what question do they think you answer.

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:10.100
That's a really great way to make
sure that you've answer the question.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.890
What I be careful of is getting
too much feedback there or

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:16.570
having other people guide your process.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:19.160
Anyone who starts to
tell you what we want,

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.020
what Stanford wants is
probably misguiding you.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:26.290
So really focus on whether it sounds like
you and whether it answered the question.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:28.300
And also to make sure
there's no lingo in there.

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:30.210
Have somebody who doesn't
know you're industry or

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:32.880
very much about your work,
take a look at it.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.201
Because anyone should be able
to read it and understand it and

00:19:35.201 --> 00:19:37.627
they shouldn't have to know
anything about business here in.

00:19:37.627 --> 00:19:39.880
&gt;&gt; Great advice.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:45.530
So the next question is, what if I live in
a country that has few opportunities for

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:48.200
minorities to show our skills?

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:52.560
How can I show that I'm a well qualified
applicant despite my lack of awards?

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:58.990
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all, this question makes
me a little sad and I'm really sorry that

00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:02.210
anybody feels they don't have
opportunities to show their skills or

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:03.470
be their best.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:07.450
And I certainly hope that coming here,
you'd have a very different experience.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:17.471
We don't expect any specific awards,
so you don't need any to apply.

00:20:17.471 --> 00:20:22.980
I think the most important
thing to understand is

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:27.580
that we have, people who are reading your
application have extensive experience.

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:30.490
We understand different
cultures around the world,

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:32.070
different cultures within the US.

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:34.610
Different cultures within
different organizations.

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:40.550
And those environments impact what
opportunities any one of us have.

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:42.964
And just like we understand
the transcripts and

00:20:42.964 --> 00:20:45.934
grading systems are really
different all over the world.

00:20:45.934 --> 00:20:50.640
So we're going to get some insight just
based on your application about that.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:53.310
And we'll try to calibrate
our reading accordingly and

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:56.540
understand the context in
which you're operating.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:59.430
And the other thing is sometimes it
comes through in the references.

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:03.060
Sometimes references give us an idea
about the context you're in and

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:05.540
what challenges you may
have in that context.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:07.630
And that can be very useful as well.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:08.130
&gt;&gt; Fantastic.

00:21:09.620 --> 00:21:11.720
Here is another question
about a candidate's age.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:16.910
This candidate says I'm
a 40 plus applicant.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:22.499
Is there anything important for
a candidate in this age group to consider?

00:21:22.499 --> 00:21:26.200
&gt;&gt; I don't think so.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:28.390
So age isn't one of
the factors we consider,

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:33.790
what we're looking at is
what have you contributed?

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:37.080
How are you performing at
whatever level you're in?

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.520
So whether you're currently
a college senior or

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:43.800
you're two years out or five years out or
20 years out or whatever it is.

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:49.300
We want to see what you're actually
accomplishing and what you're impact is.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.930
And we might expect slightly more impact

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:55.120
from somebody later in their career than
earlier in their career just because

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.170
they've had more opportunity
to create such impact.

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:01.667
That's really the only thing
that comes to mind for me,

00:22:01.667 --> 00:22:04.300
I think people ask a lot
about the average age.

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:07.849
And they forget that that's really
a function not of our decision process but

00:22:07.849 --> 00:22:10.780
of who applies and
people are typically applying.

00:22:10.780 --> 00:22:12.060
And a different stage of their career, but

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:15.590
we're really excited to see anyone
at any stage of their career.

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:17.730
It's just one more kind of
diversity we can add to the class.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:22.110
&gt;&gt; Great, so
here's a question from someone who has

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.740
already applied to the program and
is reapplying.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:31.070
Any advice for re-applicants who
did not get interviewed in general?

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:33.150
Any general advice for re-applicants?

00:22:33.150 --> 00:22:36.870
&gt;&gt; Sure, well first of all,
we welcome re-applicants.

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:41.130
I was talking to a student who told me it
was his fourth time that he got in and

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:42.540
he's having a great experience.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:47.460
And the reality of it is that we only
have such a few number of seats in

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:49.770
the class for
all to the number of people applying.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:54.150
And so we have to turn away
great candidates every year.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:57.210
And we welcome you to come back and
take another try.

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:02.560
We will look at your application
fresh in the pool of

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:04.940
candidates that are applying
when you apply.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:09.160
So we're not looking back at your previous
one and we don't want you too either.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:10.890
We want you to really take it fresh.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:14.670
An essay like what matters most to you and
why may not have changed.

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:17.150
It may have changed because
you've thought more deeply.

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:20.880
But you can certainly reuse an essay like
that, but you want fresh recommendations.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.930
You really want to approach
the whole process fresh.

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.200
Don't let the fact that
you applied before and

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:29.920
had a negative outcome
dominate your process.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:32.860
Just think of yourself as a new
applicant just like we would.

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:35.040
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:39.190
So the next question is relevant to
candidates who have experience in

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:39.880
the military.

00:23:41.340 --> 00:23:45.870
How does the admissions committee consider
the role of mandatory military service?

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:48.933
Particularly if it
occurred before college?

00:23:48.933 --> 00:23:53.734
And then, second part of that question
is what is the single most common

00:23:53.734 --> 00:23:58.633
mistake that military applicants
make during the admissions process?

00:23:58.633 --> 00:24:02.850
So we're aware that in certain countries
there's mandatory military service.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:06.660
And we see obviously from every
applicant in that country.

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:09.770
Or in some countries every male
applicant in that country,

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:11.730
we'll see that mandatory service.

00:24:11.730 --> 00:24:14.070
We are interested in what
you did during that time.

00:24:14.070 --> 00:24:18.250
A lot of times it can be
a great opportunity to explore

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:19.105
your leadership skills.

00:24:19.105 --> 00:24:23.560
And/or observe other people in leadership
roles in a different way than you do in

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:24.270
daily life.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:30.780
But we don't look at it any differently
than any other application.

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.240
So we're still looking for

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:36.510
the same criteria in all candidates
regardless of what their specific path is.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:41.360
And in terms of a single most common
mistake from military applicants,

00:24:41.360 --> 00:24:43.070
I don't think there is one.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:46.520
People are so different regardless of
what background they're coming from,

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:51.120
so I don't think there's
any one thing I see there.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:55.230
We have lots of strong applicants,
nothing comes to mind.

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:59.059
&gt;&gt; Okay, I think we have time for
just a couple more questions.

00:25:00.070 --> 00:25:04.670
So this next one is about
diversity here on campus.

00:25:04.670 --> 00:25:05.280
The question is,

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:10.370
how does the GSB work to create an
intentionally diverse learning community?

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:13.760
How do applicants best demonstrate
their diverse backgrounds and

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:16.009
experiences in their application?

00:25:17.655 --> 00:25:21.310
&gt;&gt; Paul, it's an exciting question it's
just one of the big challenges for us,

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:25.400
and an exciting one because we
really have the opportunity

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:29.000
to put together a class where people
get to know so much about each other.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.830
And frankly, they learn lots of things
about each other that we didn't know in

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.720
the application process too.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:39.228
And I think where people sometimes
get hung up here is that they very

00:25:39.228 --> 00:25:41.870
narrowly define diversity.

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:43.540
And for us we look at it very broadly.

00:25:43.540 --> 00:25:48.110
So every aspect of who
people are comes into play.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:53.010
And yeah, there's no categories or
anything.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:56.640
So how to demonstrate your background and
experience.

00:25:57.770 --> 00:26:00.650
You have opportunity throughout
the application to just, again,

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:04.270
all you have to do is answer
the questions and it just shows up.

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:08.650
I think sometimes people are applying,
and they know other people are applying.

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:11.130
And they think,
how can I possibly distinguish myself?

00:26:11.130 --> 00:26:11.850
We're all the same.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:16.910
But the thing is, you're most likely to
be most like the people you know already.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:20.720
They might be working in your company, in
your industry, they may be your friends,

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:23.600
but we're actually seeing a much bigger,
broader pool.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:28.080
And so, we don't necessarily
see that as a challenge.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:32.667
And this year in our More About You
section there's actually an opportunity to

00:26:32.667 --> 00:26:35.727
specifically share how you
think your background or

00:26:35.727 --> 00:26:39.220
your perspective has had
an impact on your experiences.

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:40.850
So that's another place as well.

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:42.180
&gt;&gt; Great point.

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:44.710
So, let's look at the last question
that we've taken this session.

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:51.690
Elizabeth, are students that apply for
deferred admission evaluated separately or

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:56.610
differently from those applying for
direct enrollment?

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:57.700
&gt;&gt; No, they're not.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.310
That said, so

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:05.330
basically all candidates are reviewed
together in the round in which they apply.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:10.996
But the one difference is that obviously
we have a set number of spots for

00:27:10.996 --> 00:27:15.790
next years' class, and so
as we get to round three,

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:19.880
we have much fewer seats to offer,
it gets more competitive.

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:24.780
But for deferred admission,
we don't have that limitation, and so for

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:28.380
college seniors, anybody applying
that qualifies to deferred admission,

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:32.970
you can actually apply and In round three,
because it's not going to be more

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:36.890
competitive then, it'll be about
the same throughout the rounds.

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:37.560
That's about it.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:41.680
The application process and
the evaluation process are identical.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:42.200
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:44.090
Well, we're almost out of time.

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:47.090
If you didn't get your
question answered today,

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:49.610
feel free to contact our office by email.

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:52.340
Now, or phoned to ask your question.

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:55.110
Before we go, Elizabeth, any last word for

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:57.660
our listeners today in terms of
how to be a great applicant?

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:03.590
&gt;&gt; Sure, my one last thought is that one
thing our students really have in common

